1. Patient Details

Patient Initials / Identifier: R.M.

Age: 62 years

Gender: Male

Weight: 82 kg

2. Suspected Adverse Reaction

Description of Reaction: Severe bilateral thigh/calf myalgia, extreme fatigue, dark (tea-colored) urine, and elevated serum Creatine Kinase (>10x upper limit of normal).

Date of Reaction Onset: Day 5 after starting Fluconazole.

Severity: Severe / High Risk (rhabdomyolysis requiring emergency care and IV hydration).

3. Suspected Medication(s)

Primary Suspect: Atorvastatin 40 mg daily (orally) for Hyperlipidemia.

Interacting Agent: Fluconazole 200 mg daily (orally) for fungal infection.

Treatment Timeline: Atorvastatin started 1 month prior; Fluconazole added 5 days before symptom onset.

Action Taken: Both medications immediately discontinued upon ED presentation.

4. Concomitant Medications

Medication: Metformin 500 mg twice daily (taken for 5 years for Type 2 Diabetes; no interaction).

5. Outcome & Action Taken

Action Taken: Immediate withdrawal of Atorvastatin and Fluconazole; IV fluid therapy initiated.

De-challenge Outcome: Muscle pain subsided, urine color normalized, and CK levels returned to baseline within 14 days.

Re-challenge: Not re-challenged with the combination. Patient restarted on an alternative lipid-lowering agent after resolution.

Patient Outcome: Recovered completely without permanent renal damage.
